<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deeg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dig</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Deeg is a town in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, north of Bharatpur. It was a stronghold of the Jat rulers of Bharatpur, fortified by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badan-singh">Badan Singh</a> in the early 18th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suraj-mal">Suraj Mal</a> built its water palaces, a garden complex of pavilions, tanks and fountains. In 1804, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-maratha-war">Second Anglo-Maratha War</a>, the British won a battle there against Holkar's army.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deeg.jpg" alt="Deeg" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sainiikshank1410 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palace_of_Deeg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
